WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Authentication flow selection depends on the application type and trust boundary.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:19.720
A server-side web application, a web API, a desktop tool, a command-line utility, a background daemon, and a single-page application have different abilities to protect credentials and different expectations for user interaction.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.320
Authorization code flow is commonly used for ASP.NET Core web applications that perform interactive sign-in.

4
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:38.660
The application redirects the user to the identity platform, receives a response at a registered redirect URI, and establishes an application session after successful validation.

5
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:46.520
Public clients and browser-based applications have different constraints than confidential server-side clients because they cannot protect credentials in the same way.

6
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.360
Client credentials flow is for app-only access without a signed-in user.

7
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:57.460
It is appropriate when a daemon service, background process, or workload acts as itself.

8
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:05.960
On-behalf-of flow is used when an API receives a delegated user token and needs to call another downstream API while preserving that user context.

9
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:14.380
Device code flow can support limited-input devices or command-line tools, but it should be selected only when that user experience and risk profile make sense.

10
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.380
Implicit flow should not be treated as the modern default for new .NET applications.

11
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:27.800
Developers should use maintained libraries such as MSAL and Microsoft.Identity.Web rather than manually constructing protocol exchanges.

12
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:36.060
Those libraries help with standards details, token validation integration, cache behavior, error handling, and ongoing platform changes.

13
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.940
The secure design question is not just whether sign-in works.

14
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:52.040
It is whether the selected flow matches the client type, whether credentials can be protected, whether consent and permissions are appropriate, and whether downstream API calls preserve the right security context.
